   "source": [
    "# Quiz: Translation and sentiment analysis with ML\n",
    "\n",
    "> **On Alps:** These are the questions of this lesson's pre-lecture and post-lecture quizzes, from the course's quiz app, with the answers the quiz app accepts."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Pre-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-3-pre-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Naive translation</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>translates words only</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A naive translation program translates words only and ignores the sentence structure.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>translates sentence structure</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A naive program ignores the sentence structure. It only translates the words.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>translates sentiment</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Naive translation only translates the words. That leads to bad, sometimes funny, translations.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-3-pre-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>A <em>corpus</em> of texts refers to</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>A small number of texts</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A corpus means lots of text, not a small amount.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>A large number of texts</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. A corpus is lots of text. Machine learning can use it to find patterns, for example in translation.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>One standard text</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. A corpus is not one standard text. It means lots of text.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-3-pre-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>If a ML model has enough human translations to build a model on, it can</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>abbreviate translations</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. Learning from many human translations helps the model make its translations more accurate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>standardize translations</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The model looks for common patterns in human translations so it can make translations more accurate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>improve the accuracy of translations</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. By finding common patterns in texts that experts already translated, the model makes translations more accurate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Post-lecture quiz"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-3-post-1\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>Underlying TextBlob's translation library is:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Google Translate</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Behind the scenes, TextBlob's translation used Google Translate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>Bing</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. TextBlob's translation ran on Google Translate behind the scenes.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>A custom ML model</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. TextBlob did not use its own model here. Behind the scenes, it used Google Translate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-3-post-2\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>To use <c>blob.translate</c> you need:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>an internet connection</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. blob.translate used Google Translate behind the scenes, so it needed an internet connection. TextBlob 0.18 removed this feature.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>a dictionary</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. blob.translate relied on Google Translate behind the scenes, so it needed an internet connection, not a dictionary.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>JavaScript</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. blob.translate comes from the TextBlob library. It needed an internet connection, since it used Google Translate.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "raw",
   "metadata": {
    "format": "application/vnd.pretext+xml"
   },
   "source": [
    "<exercise label=\"mlb-quiz-6-3-post-3\">\n",
    "  <statement>\n",
    "    <p>To determine sentiment, an ML approach would be to:</p>\n",
    "  </statement>\n",
    "  <choices randomize=\"no\">\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>apply Regression techniques to manually generated opinions and scores and look for patterns</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The lesson's ML approach applies NLP techniques to the opinions and scores, so patterns emerge.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice correct=\"yes\">\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>apply NLP techniques to manually generated opinions and scores and look for patterns</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Correct. Gather texts where people gave a score and an opinion, then apply NLP techniques so patterns emerge.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "    <choice>\n",
    "      <statement>\n",
    "        <p>apply Clustering techniques to manually generated opinions and scores and look for patterns</p>\n",
    "      </statement>\n",
    "      <feedback>\n",
    "        <p>Not quite. The lesson applies NLP techniques here, so patterns emerge, like positive movie reviews saying 'Oscar worthy' more often.</p>\n",
    "      </feedback>\n",
    "    </choice>\n",
    "  </choices>\n",
    "</exercise>"
   ]
  }
